00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.200
Ivan, what is Cloud
Native Development?

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.410
IVAN PORTYANKIN: So when
we talk about Cloud Native

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.640
we can discuss the
six major principles.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.140
First, we build everything
as microservices.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.010
So instead of big
[INAUDIBLE] applications

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.940
split into small
microservices, which

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.190
use different technologies
for those microservices.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.990
We track them separately
in source repositories.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.490
And we can scale
them granularly.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.550
Second, everything
should be code.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.580
This way we can track this code,
we can roll back this code,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.230
and see the version of change.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.290
This includes source code, test
code, and automation scripts.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.250
Third, everything now should
be billed as containers.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.830
Within a container image you
can include all dependencies

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.470
and tools that you
need and focus on code

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.560
itself, and not worry about
system incompatibilities

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.910
[INAUDIBLE] complex.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.270
Fourth is we need automation.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.500
Since we split our system
into small microservices,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.990
complexity went up.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.880
And we need to take
care of moving pieces

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.030
by using [INAUDIBLE] integration
system, building on each

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.360
commit to make sure integration
runs, and all pieces,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.520
moving pieces, and
microservices are well

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.945
tested against each other.

00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:51.880
Fifth is declarative approach.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.960
So for Kubernetes, the
system state is complicated.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.180
So instead of scripts,
we write a YAML

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.100
where we describe the
state of the system.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.400
The desired state in Kubernetes
drives the system to that state

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.570
and takes care of it.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.080
And sixth and last,
scalable system.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.680
In Kubernetes
world, it means you

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.850
can scale your microservices
running as a single deployment

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.200
to multiple replicates.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.320
In a serverless system
it's even more important

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.200
you pay for what you use.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.280
You pay on demand.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.790
So whenever you have a
request, we can burst up

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.440
or burst down your
deployment, and make sure

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.000
we can serve your resources.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.560
SARAH D'ANGELO: And there are
essentially two approaches

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.820
for Cloud Native Development.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.980
We have Kubernetes
and serverless.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.630
And it really boils
down to how much

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.120
control you want over the
stack, versus how much you want

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.240
to focus on the business logic.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.810
Serverless is
incredibly powerful,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.352
and it lets you
focus on the business

00:02:38.352 --> 00:02:40.080
logic of your
application by providing

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.160
a higher level of abstraction.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.830
Kubernetes, on the
other hand, gives you

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.550
a higher granularity and control
over the stack implementation.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.210
And whichever one you choose,
we've got you covered.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.760
Google provides end to end
tooling for both serverless

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.210
and Kubernetes.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.750
In this talk, we're going
to focus on Cloud Code.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.110
So it'll be centering on the
coding part of this journey.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.810
So what is Cloud Code?

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.470
Cloud Code is our IDE plugin
for Visual Studio code

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.072
and JetBrains IDEs.

00:03:08.072 --> 00:03:09.780
And our goal with
Cloud Code is to really

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.940
make your inner development
[INAUDIBLE] easy.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.170
We're going to speed up
your development experience,

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.980
simplify local development,
extend the production

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.570
deployments, and allow you to
debug, deploy applications,

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:23.405
all from in your IDE.

00:03:23.405 --> 00:03:24.780
And we understand
that developers

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.440
have many different
workflows, and Cloud Code

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.870
can support you wherever
you are in your journey.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.420
Whether it's getting started
with easy to use templates,

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.090
migrating your application
to Kubernetes, editing

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:38.630
an existing Cloud Run or
Kubernetes application,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.300
or containerizing
application, we've

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.790
got you covered with a lot of
great features and resources

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.360
with Cloud Code.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.830
So let's dive right
in and show you

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:51.500
